906cd98c", "line_end": 8136, "line_start": 8129}, "owner_tag": "0042", "parent_tag": "0042", "semantic_path": "paragraph/0005", "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_unit": {"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0042", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:e11e36da1276b2cd16e3e6b5120e5441fdb8649b1f30fdc93d9ed136d35b4812", "source_text": "<STRUCT_0001>\nFor this reason it is tempting to define a ``representable'' object in the\n2-category of categories fibred in groupoids to be a category fibred in\nsets whose associated presheaf is representable. However, this is would not\nbe a good definition for use since we prefer to have a notion which is\ninvariant under equivalences. To make this precise we study exactly\nwhich categories fibred in groupoids are equivalent to categories\nfibred in sets.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f33b15bcaff218162f76e9666285051808fdf6de2c53efbbe30f6be9c95c2401", "unit_id": "inventory:categories:0042:paragraph/0005"}, "state": "READY"}, "reference:categories-example-category-over-X": {"context_hash": "sha256:ee2074c7afc9aafdc3d52c6aab074190672eb69acc5751462659531b2203f03e", "kind": "locked-English-reference", "location": {"byte_end": 10514, "byte_start": 9795, "file": "categories.tex", "fragment_hash": "sha256:9a33bf70b81ec79911a7ad97a20802b8740e8c3982b1314e1b68833a49c86626", "line_end": 268, "line_start": 254}, "owner_tag": "001G", "parent_tag": "0013", "requested_label": "categories-example-category-over-X", "requested_tag": "001G", "semantic_path": "example/0001", "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_unit": {"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "